{{en-adj|-}}

# {{senseid|en|pertaining to the Roman god Neptune}} {{lb|en|Roman mythology}} Of or pertaining to [[Neptune]], the [[Roman#Adjective|Roman]] [[god]] of [[fresh water]] and the [[sea]], the [[counterpart]] of the [[Greek#Adjective|Greek]] god [[Poseidon]].
#: {{nearsyn|en|Poseidonian<id:pertaining to the Greek god Poseidon>}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Lucius Annaeus Seneca [''i.e.'', {{w|Seneca the Younger}}]|translator=w:Edward Sherburne|title=[[w:The Trojan Women|Troades: Or The Royal Captives. A Tragedy, Written Originally in Latin,{{nb...|by Lucius Annæus Seneca, the Philosopher. English’d by Edward Sherburne, Esq; with Annotations.}}]]|location=London|publisher=Printed by Anne Godbid, and {{w|John Playford}}, for Samuel Carr,{{nb...|at the Kings-Head in St. Paul’s Church-Yard.}}|year=1679|section=act II, scene i|page=21|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=u0RoAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA21|oclc=228722854|passage=The Tomb disburd'ning, whence the Ghoſt aroſe / Of great ''{{w|Achilles}}''; Such when ''Thracian'' Foes / (The Prelude of thy Fates, ''Troy''!) he o'rethrew, / And the white hair'd '''''Neptunian''' Cycnus'' [''i.e.'', Neptune's son] ſlew.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension|rare}} Of or pertaining to [[water#Noun|water]] or the sea.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:John Dyer|chapter=The Ruins of Rome. A Poem.|title=Poems. [...] Viz. I. Grongar Hill. II. The Ruins of Rome. III. The Fleece, in Four Books|location=London|publisher=Printed by John Hughs, for Messrs. [[w:Robert Dodsley|R[obert]]] and [[w:James Dodsley|J[ames] Dodsley]],{{nb...|in Pallmall.}}|year=1740|year_published=1759|pages=42–43|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=A0gVAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA42|oclc=991281870|passage=Tyrian garbs, / '''Neptunian''' Albion's high teſtaceous food [''i.e.'', oysters], / And flavour'd Chian wines with incenſe fum'd / To ſlake Patrician thirſt: for theſe, their rights / In the vile ſtreets they proſtitute to ſale; / Their ancient rights, their dignities, their laws, / Their native glorious freedom.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=G. D.|title=The Tar’s Engagement|editor=Sylvanus Urban [pseudonym; {{w|Edward Cave}}]|magazine=[[w:The Gentleman's Magazine|The Gentleman’s Magazine, and Historical Chronicle]]|location=London|publisher=Printed{{nb...|at St John’s Gate}}, for D[avid] Henry, and sold by [[w:Francis Newbery (publisher)|F[rancis] Newbery]],{{nb...|the corner of St. Paul’s Church-Yard, Ludgate-Street.}}|month=November|year=1774|volume=XLIV|page=534|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=mHpIAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA534|column=1|oclc=192374019|passage=Where the inſulting ſtreams of Chaos roar, / Where boiſt'rous waves the rugged ſhores ſurround, / And ſtorms and whirlwinds in the air abound, / There meet two rivals of '''Neptunian''' race, / Hoſtilities begin with fierce grimace: [...]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Horace|chapter=[[w:Odes (Horace)|Ode]] IX. To [[w:Gaius Maecenas|Mæcenas]].|translator=William Smart|title=Horace Literally Translated for the Use of Students|location=London|publisher=Printed for Whittaker, Treacher & Co.,{{nb...|Ave-Maria Lane.}}|year=1830|page=105|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=nucIAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA105|oclc=1003871256|passage=Since lately the '''Neptunian''' admiral (''[[w:Sextus Pompey|Sextus Pompeius]]'') when driven from the sea, fled with his burnt ships, having menaced those chains to Rome, which as a friend he had taken off from perfidious slaves.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension|geology}} [[form#Verb|Formed]] by the [[action]] of water.
#: {{ux|en|'''neptunian''' dyke}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Martin Hovland|chapter=Ancient and Modern Analogues|title=Deep-water Coral Reefs: Unique Biodiversity Hot-spots|series=Springer–Praxis Books in Life Sciences Incorporating Aquatic and Marine Sciences|location=Dordrecht; Berlin|publisher=[[w:Springer Science+Business Media|Springer]]; Chichester, West Sussex: Praxis Publishing|year=2008|section=section 7.3.2 (Kess-Kess Formations, Morocco)|page=170|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=CIc7w6SuhY8C&pg=PA170|isbn=978-1-4020-8461-4|passage=A special character of these mounds [the Kess-Kess carbonate mud mounds] is the numerous '''Neptunian''' dikes. These are fracture systems, which have been filled with other sediments and minerals. It is suspected that these dikes represent spring conduits (seeps) during the formation of the mounds, and that the seeping fluids were of a hydrothermal nature [...].}}
# {{lb|en|by extension|geology|historical}} Of, pertaining to, or [[support#Verb|supporting]] {{l|en|Neptunism|t=a [[discredited#Adjective|discredited]] [[theory]] that [[rock#Noun|rocks]] were formed from the [[crystallisation]] of [[mineral]]s in the early [[Earth]]'s [[ocean]]s}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:John Murray (physician)|[John Murray]]]|chapter=Part III. Of the Arguments in Support of the Huttonian and Neptunian Theories, from the Positions of the Strata of the Globe.|title=A Comparative View of the Huttonian and Neptunian Systems of Geology: In Answer to the Illustrations of the Huttonian Theory of the Earth, by Professor [[w:John Playfair|[John] Playfair]]|location=Edinburgh|publisher=Printed for Ross and Blackwood,{{nb...|South Bridge Street}}; London: [[w:Longman|T. N. Longman, and G. Rees]]|year=1802|page=106|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/acomparativevie01murrgoog/page/n120/mode/1up|oclc=5894371461|passage=In the '''Neptunian''' ſyſtem it is ſuppoſed that the poſitions of the ſtrata have been determined, partly by the figure of the baſe or ground on which they have been depoſited, and partly by their depoſition having been a ſpecies of cryſtallization.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=[[w:Bernhard Studer|B[ernhard] Studer]]|title=On Mineral Metamorphism|editor=w:Robert Jameson|journal=[[w:Royal Society of Edinburgh|The Edinburgh New Philosophical Journal, Exhibiting a View of the Progressive Discoveries and Improvements in the Sciences and the Arts]]|location=Edinburgh|publisher=[[w:A & C Black|Adam & Charles Black]]; London: [[w:Longman|Longman, Brown, Green & Longmans]]|month=April|year=1848|volume=XLIV|issue=LXXXVIII|page=372|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=6x0AAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA372|oclc=926996861|passage=In several important cases, it is difficult to determine to what degree the metamorphosis has extended, since it remains uncertain whether the form and structural relations of the mountain-mass are to be considered as remains of the '''neptunian''' sedimentary formation, or as a product of the metamorphosis itself; for the original form of the '''neptunian''', as well as of the volcanic sedimentary rocks have often become so changed by erosion, and so much alike in both classes of rocks, that this distinctive character is wholly lost; [...]}}

