# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[sailor]].
#: {{synonyms|en|Neptunist|Thesaurus:sailor}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:John Taylor (poet)|John Taylor]]|title=The Praise of Hemp-seed: With the Voyage of Mr. Roger Bird and the Writer hereof, in a Boat of Brown-paper, from London to Quinborough in Kent.{{nb...|As also, a Farewell to the Matchlesse Deceased Mr. Thomas Coriat. Concluding with the Commendations of the Famous Riuer of Thames. By Iohn Taylor. The Contents of the Booke are in the Next Leafe before the Preamble. The Profits Arising by Hemp-seed are Cloathing, Food, Fishing, Shipping, Pleasure, Profit, Iustice, Whipping}}|location=printed at London|publisher=For H. Gosson,{{nb...|and are to be sold at Christ-Church gate}}|year=1620|oclc=837721081|newversion=republished in|title2=All the Workes of Iohn Taylor the Water Poet:{{nb...|Being 63 in Number Collected into One Volum by the Author with Sundry New Additions, Corrected, Revised, and Newly Imprinted.}}|location2=London|publisher2=Printed by I[ohn] B[eale, [[w:Edward Allde|Elizabeth Allde]], Bernard Alsop, and Thomas Fawcet] for Iames Boler;{{nb...|at the signe of the Marigold in Pauls Churchyard}}|year2=1630|page2=66|pageurl2=https://books.google.com/books?id=_RRfAAAAcAAJ&pg=RA3-PA66|column2=2|oclc2=1049101215|passage=You braue '''''Neptunians''''', you ſalt water crew, / Sea-plowing Marriners; I ſpeake to you: [...]}}
# {{lb|en|geology|historical}} A [[proponent]] of [[Neptunism]].
#: {{synonyms|en|Neptunist}}
#: {{antonyms|en|Plutonian|Plutonist|plutonist|volcanist|Vulcanist}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=''View of the Mineralogy, Agriculture, Manufactures, and Fisheries, of the Island of Arran,'' {{...|with Notices of Antiquities, and Suggestions for Improving the Agriculture and Fisheries of the Highlands and Isles of Scotland,}} by the Rev. James Headrick. pp. 395. Price 10s. 6d. Constable and Co. Edinburgh: Murray, London, 1807. [book review]|magazine=The Literary Panorama. Being a Review of Books, Magazine of Varieties, and Annual Register|location=London|publisher=Printed by Cox, Son, and Baylis,{{nb...|Great Queen-Street}}, [f]or [[w:Charles Taylor (engraver)|C[harles] Taylor]],{{nb...|No. 108, Hatton Garden, Holborn.}}|month=October|year=1807|year_published=March 1808|volume=III|column=57|columnurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=h2Q3AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA57|oclc=176276973|passage=We are not ourselves, ''bigotted'' '''Neptunians''' or Vulcanians: we perceive difficulties whichever theory be adopted: and provided the earth be but stable under us, we are not anxiously jealous for the dignity of the trident-wielding power, or for the honour of the hammer-working deity.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:John Mason Good; w:Olinthus Gregory; Newton Bosworth|entry=OBSIDIAN|entryurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=OMo6AQAAMAAJ&pg=PP566|title=[[w:Pantologia|Pantologia. A New Cyclopædia, Comprehending a Complete Series of Essays, Treatises, and Systems, Alphabetically Arranged;{{nb...|with a General Dictionary of Arts, Sciences, and Words: The Whole Presenting a Distinct Survey of Human Genius, Learning, and Industry. Illustrated with Elegant Engravings; those on Natural History being from Original Drawings by Edwards and Others, and Beautifully Coloured after Nature.}}]]|location=London|publisher=Printed for G. Kearsley; [''et al.'']|year=1813|volume=VIII (MID–OZO)|column=1|oclc=733059775|passage=Its [obsidian's] origin is warmly contested between the '''Neptunians''' and Plutonians, many of the latter denominating it a vitreous lava.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Art. IV.—''An Elementary Treatise on Mineralogy and Geology, being an Introduction to the Study of Those Sciences,'' {{...|and Designed for the Use of Pupils, for Persons Attending Lectures on These Subjects, and as a Companion for Travellers in the United States of America. Illustrated by Six Plates.}} By {{w|Parker Cleaveland}}, Professor of Mathematics and Natural Philosophy, and Lecturer in Chymistry and Mineralogy in Bowdoin College, &c. &c. Boston, 1816. Large 8vo. pp. 668.|magazine=[[w:Analectic Magazine|The Analectic Magazine]]|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher=Published by Moses Thomas|month=April|year=1817|volume=IX|pages=312–313|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=3KA6AQAAIAAJ&pg=PA313|oclc=780179261|passage=As to the two theories, neither will suffice alone. The neptunian is utterly inadequate to explain the phenomena of basalt; nor can a vulcanist account for the appearance of an hydrophane.—We need both explanations. Those geologists who have travelled in the north of Europe, are generally '''neptunians'''; those who have made their observations in the south of the same regions, are generally vulcanists.}}

