===Etymology 2===
[[File:Neptune Voyager2 color calibrated.png|thumb|The [[planet]] [[Neptune]] photographed in 1989 by the US [[w:NASA|National Aeronautics and Space Administration]]’s [[space probe]] {{w|Voyager 2}}. The word ''Neptunian'' also means “of or pertaining to the planet” ''(etymology 2, adjective sense 2)'' as well as “an ([[imaginary]]) [[inhabitant]] of the planet” ''(noun sense 2)''.]]

From {{suffix|en|Neptune|t1=eighth planet in the solar system|ian}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|-}}

# {{lb|en|astrology}} Pertaining to the [[astrological]] [[influence#Noun|influence]] of the [[planet]] Neptune.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Liz Greene; Howard Sasportas|chapter=Your Inborn Images|title=The Development of the Personality: Seminars in Psychological Astrology|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher=[[w:Red Wheel/Weiser/Conari|Red Wheel/Weiser]]|year=1987|volume=1|section=part 1 (The Stages of Childhood)|pages=11–12|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=d3HQRMjUy1wC&pg=PA11|isbn=978-0-87728-673-8|passage=The Sun represents a masculine principle—assertion, expression and spirit. Neptune touching it brings in the qualities of sensitivity and creativity as well as weakness, dissolution, dissipation and elusiveness. If we take the Sun to represent the image of father, the person's experience of father (the Sun) will be coloured by Neptune. The father will receive the '''Neptunian''' projection and the child with this aspect will be sensitive to the father's '''Neptunian''' side.}}
# {{lb|en|astronomy}} Of or pertaining to the planet Neptune.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=[[w:Sears Cook Walker|Sears C[ook] Walker]]|title=Researches Relative to the Planet Neptune|journal=[[w:Smithsonian Contributions and Studies Series|Smithsonian Contributions to Knowledge]]|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=Published by the {{w|Smithsonian Institution}}|year=1851|volume=II|section=2|page=17|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=AY05AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA17|oclc=608756746|passage=After making a sketch of the '''Neptunian''' regions for the dates from 1790 to 1800, I soon came to the conclusion that the nights of the 8th and 10th of May, 1795, were the only ones that afforded a reasonable prospect of furnishing an observation of Neptune, and accordingly computed for the evening of the 10th of May, the limits of the '''Neptunian''' region, or in other words the ''locus'' of Neptune as a fixed star, [...]}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=[[w:Alexander Winchell|[Alexander Winchell]]]|title=Art. V.—The Unity of the Physical World.|editor=D. D. Whedon|magazine=Methodist Quarterly Review|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Nelson & Phillips; Cincinnati, Oh.: Hitchcock & Walden|month=January|year=1874|volume=XXVI (4th Series; volume LVI overall)|section=section II (Facts of Succession), subsection I (Primordial History of the Solar System)|page=83|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ISdGAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA83|oclc=|passage=The planet Uranus, in like manner, detached, in process of time, a satellite, followed, in the progress of the cosmic ages, by seven others. A slow disturbing influence, supposed also to be recognized in the '''Neptunian''' system, has manifested itself in the Uranian, in an excessive obliquity of the plane of the Uranian satellites—an obliquity which considerably exceeds a right angle, [...]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Carolyn Porco|Carolyn C. Porco]]; [[w:Phil Nicholson|Philip D. Nicholson]]; Jeffrey N. Cuzzi; [[w:Jack J. Lissauer|Jack J[onathan] Lissauer]]; w:Larry W. Esposito|chapter=Neptune’s Ring System|editors=[[w:Dale Cruikshank|Dale P. Cruikshank]], with M. S. Matthews and A. M. Schumann|title=Neptune and Triton|series=Space Science Series|location=Tucson, Ariz.|publisher=w:University of Arizona Press|year=1995|page=733|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=My8J0G3R1-4C&pg=PA733|isbn=978-0-8165-1525-7|passage=For the '''Neptunian''' rings, we again expect that <math>Q_{occ} = 2</math> for Earth-based occultation data, so long as the mean particle size is substantially larger than the wavelength of observation (2.2μm). [...] In fact, photometric observations suggest that most '''Neptunian''' ring particles are sub-centimeter in size [...], so that we have <math>Q_{occ} \simeq 2</math>.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Helen Klus|chapter=The Planet Neptune|title=How We Came to Know the Cosmos: Space & Time|location=[United Kingdom]|publisher=The Star Garden|year=2017|section=part III (Missions to Planets), paragraph 24.2|page=220|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=4wRMDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA220|isbn=978-1-9998778-0-4|passage=Neptune has a faint ring system and 14 known moons. The largest of these is Triton, which contains over 90% of the mass of all the '''Neptunian''' moons, and is the only '''Neptunian''' moon that's spherical.}}

